#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""fm-memory-migrate - mirror Claude Code's auto-memory files into the lane banks.

Claude Code keys its per-project memory by filesystem path, so one project
accumulates several mutually invisible memory directories and lane-wide facts
get copy-pasted between projects. This copies those files into the Mnemosyne
lane banks, canonicalising the fragments into one project identity per project,
without touching a single source file.

MIRROR, NOT MOVE (captain decision D3, 2026-08-20)
  The markdown files stay canonical and are never read-write opened, moved,
  rewritten, or deleted. Every run records a manifest of every source file's
  size and SHA-256 before and after its writes and refuses to report success if
  any of them changed. The cutover that would make the store canonical is a
  separate, later decision.

CREDENTIALS NEVER ENTER THE STORE (captain decision D2, 2026-08-20)
  The store is served by a local dashboard, so a secret written into it is a
  secret published. A file whose text is secret-bearing is therefore never
  copied. It is indexed as a POINTER that names where the file lives and what
  it is for, and carries none of its content. Detection is deliberately
  over-inclusive - a false positive costs one pointer instead of one copy, a
  false negative publishes a credential - and every flag is reported with the
  rule and the redacted evidence that fired it, so the judgement is inspectable
  rather than opaque.

Usage:
  fm-memory-migrate plan   [--source S] [--home H] [--data-dir D] [--json]
      Derive the whole migration and print it. Opens no store, writes nothing.
  fm-memory-migrate write  [--source S] [--home H] [--data-dir D] [--json]
      Write every routed memory through fm-memory-mcp and report the counts.
  fm-memory-migrate verify [--source S] [--home H] [--data-dir D] [--json]
      Run the integrity checklist against the banks and report each check.
  fm-memory-migrate --help

ROUTING
  source dir -> project   the directory name is the source path with every
                          non-alphanumeric run collapsed to '-', which is
                          lossy, so a project is resolved by matching the
                          LONGEST registered project name that ends the
                          directory name on a '-' boundary. Every fragment of
                          one project therefore lands on that one project:
                          the four EquiMe directories become one 'EquiMe'.
  project -> lane         bin/fm-project-mode.sh --lane, which owns the
                          registry line format. A directory that matches no
                          registered project, and a registered project with no
                          lane token, are reported UNROUTED and nothing is
                          written for them. Guessing a lane is the cross-lane
                          leak the lane model exists to prevent.
  MEMORY.md               never migrated; it is a rendering of the other files'
                          descriptions and is reconstructible from them.

TWO FRONTMATTER DIALECTS
  Newer files carry `metadata.type` under a `metadata:` block; older ones carry
  a top-level `type:`. Both are parsed, and a file with neither is migrated as
  untyped session context rather than dropped.

DEDUPE (report section 5.4)
  1. Exact. SHA-256 of the normalized body - frontmatter stripped, whitespace
     collapsed, case folded. One group becomes ONE memory: under its project
     when every copy is that project's, and under the lane-wide '_lane' channel
     when copies span projects inside one lane, which is what the duplicated
     build-and-release files actually are. A group spanning two LANES is
     written once in each, flagged `cross_lane_duplicate`, because a cross-lane
     write is never allowed and picking one lane would delete the other's copy.
  2. Near. Not merged here. Cosine similarity is computed by `verify` from the
     embeddings the store itself wrote, and pairs at or above the threshold are
     REPORTED for review. Auto-merging near-duplicates across projects is how a
     lane-local fact silently becomes another project's.
  3. Pointer collision. A body whose normalized text a canonical firstmate file
     already states is dropped in favour of the pointer bin/fm-memory-pointers
     wrote; the canonical file always wins. Only an exact normalized match
     drops a memory. A partial overlap is reported instead, because dropping on
     a fuzzy signal loses content that nothing else holds.

IDEMPOTENCE
  Re-running writes no new rows. Content is derived deterministically, so an
  unchanged file produces the exact text already stored and the store's own
  dedupe returns the same row: reported `created: false` and counted skipped.
  A ledger beside each bank records each memory's id and content digest, so a
  file that actually CHANGED is written as a supersession of the recorded row
  rather than as a second live memory claiming the same thing.
  The ledger is derived, never authoritative: if it is lost, or the row it
  names is gone or already retired, the memory is written fresh instead.
  Which record belongs to a memory is decided by the source files that record
  carries, not by the key it sits under. A record whose files are not this
  memory's is another memory's row and is never superseded, so a same-stem
  sibling that goes missing can never hand its neighbour's key to anyone.

WHAT THIS DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO
  It never retires a memory whose source file disappeared. Under mirror mode a
  missing file is as likely to be a renamed or unmounted directory as a
  deletion, and mass-retiring a lane on that reading is unrecoverable. Such
  ledger keys are reported as `orphaned` so the loss of the link is visible,
  and retiring them stays a deliberate act. Both `write` and `verify` report
  them, and the count reconciliation reads the store's live rows as this run's
  memories PLUS those kept orphans rather than calling them a mismatch.
  It does not move a memory to another channel of its own lane.
  A fact a second project of the lane takes up belongs to the lane-wide
  channel, and one no longer shared belongs back under its project, but that
  move cannot be expressed through the bridge: its dedupe is keyed on the
  session and the content and ignores the channel, so identical content under
  a new channel matches the row that already holds it.
  `memory_supersede` reads that as superseding a memory by itself and refuses
  `supersede_noop`; `memory_remember` refuses `duplicate_in_other_project`.
  So the write is refused, `write` exits nonzero, and `verify` fails check 15
  naming the memory and both channels - the bound is loud and checked, never
  silent. Relocation needs a capability at the bridge, which is shipped
  phase-1 code the dashboard and Hermes also read: follow-up
  memory-bridge-channel-move-followup-q73 carries that decision.
  A ledger record and a source file are the same memory when their paths
  intersect OR their text is identical: an edit keeps the paths, a rename
  keeps the text. A file that is renamed AND edited between two runs matches
  on neither, and nothing on disk distinguishes that from the file having been
  deleted and a different one created. This tool does not guess: it treats it
  as exactly that, so the old memory is kept and reported as an orphan and the
  new file becomes a new memory. That is an accepted bound of mirror mode, not
  an oversight - merging them would mean retiring a memory on a guess.
  It writes no graph_edges for `[[wikilinks]]`: every write goes through the
  bridge, the bridge exposes no edge tool, and reaching around it into sqlite
  is exactly the direct-store access this whole design refuses. Link targets
  are preserved in `metadata.links` and in the body text itself.

ONE CONTENT MODEL, THREE NAMES
  `group_key` is the folded digest that decides whether two files say the same
  thing, and it groups duplicates and nothing else.
  `identity_digest` is the exact-byte digest that says whether a ledger record
  and a derived entry are the same memory across time, beside the source paths
  they share; `same_memory` is the one place that question is answered.
  `stored_form` is the text the store actually holds, produced by the store's
  own sanitizer, and `holds` is the one place materialisation is answered -
  whether a bank row carries this entry's current text.
  Identity and materialisation never borrow each other's implementation: a row
  carrying the id the ledger recorded proves something once landed, not that
  what landed is what the file says now.
  A stored form is its own type and refuses to be compared with a raw string:
  the store rewrites some content before it writes, so a raw comparison reads
  a correct migration as a lost memory, and a comparison that answered False
  quietly is the shape of every defect this model exists to prevent.

THE BRIDGE IS THE ONLY WRITER
  Nothing here opens a store read-write. Every write is a tools/call on a
  `fm-memory-mcp serve` process, so the pin against the 24h trim, the global
  scope, the lane binding, and every typed refusal are the bridge's and are not
  reimplemented. `verify` opens banks read-only, in mode=ro, to prove what
  actually landed rather than trusting what was reported.
  The stdio client below is deliberately its own small class rather than one
  shared with bin/fm-memory-pointers: the bridge's stdio JSON-RPC is a
  published interface, and the two tools' failure isolation has to stay
  independent of each other.

FAILURE ISOLATION
  A lane whose bridge refuses to start costs that lane's memories and no
  others. The run continues and reports exactly which memories did not land,
  because stopping at the first bad bank leaves how much of the fleet is
  indexed unknowable.

COUNTS
  written, skipped_duplicate, updated, credential_pointers, unrouted, refused,
  dropped_for_pointer.

HERMES IS NEVER WRITTEN
  The safety property is that no memory this migration writes ever lands in
  Hermes's own bank, and `verify` asserts exactly that: it reads that bank
  read-only and looks for rows carrying this migration's sources or its
  metadata marker. Its mtime and row counts are recorded as context only,
  never as a verdict, because Hermes writes to that bank constantly and no
  before/after equality over state someone else is writing proves anything.

REFUSALS ARE RECORDED, NEVER INFERRED
  A write that did not land is written down beside the ledger, so `verify`
  reads what happened instead of guessing it from what landed. Nothing the
  bank holds distinguishes a write that was rejected from a source edited
  since the last run, and the four ways a memory can be absent need four
  different answers, so check 15 tells them apart by name.
  A STORE REFUSAL is the store rejecting the write - `duplicate_in_other_
  project`, `duplicate_is_retired`, `supersede_noop`. It is a hard failure,
  and re-running is refused identically, so a person has to act.
  A TRANSPORT FAILURE is this tool never getting to ask - `lane_unavailable`
  when the bridge would not start, `lane_lost_midrun` when its pipe died. It
  is a hard failure too, because the memory is not in the store, but running
  `write` again is the whole fix and the message says so.
  NEVER WRITTEN is no ledger record and nothing in the bank: this memory has
  not been migrated at all. A hard failure, because a memory that never
  landed reading as clean is the one thing this check exists to prevent.
  STALE is the ledger recording this memory, the bank holding it under older
  text, and the source having moved on. That is the ordinary state of a
  mirror whose sources are edited by their owners, so it is a notice rather
  than a failure - listed under `STALE` in normal output, because a state
  that is not a failure must still be impossible to miss.
  Recording a refusal never fabricates state: a lane whose bank was never
  provisioned gets no ledger written for it, because a lane minted by its own
  failure would then be read back as a bank that cannot be opened.

EXIT STATUS
  0 everything routed is durable in the store and no source file changed;
  1 anything was refused, unrouted, or a check failed; 2 usage.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import hashlib
import json
import math
import os
import re
import sqlite3
import functools
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
from datetime import datetime
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Any, Dict, Iterable, List, NamedTuple, Optional, Sequence, Set, Tuple

BRIDGE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "fm-memory-mcp"
PROJECT_MODE = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / "fm-project-mode.sh"
LEDGER_FILENAME = "fm-memory-migrate.json"
REFUSALS_FILENAME = "fm-memory-migrate-refusals.json"
SESSION = "fm:memory-migrate"
SOURCE = "claude-automemory"
POINTER_SOURCE = "claude-automemory-pointer"
MIGRATION_MARKER = "claude-automemory-phase3"
# Codes this tool records because it never reached the store, as against the
# ones the store itself issued. A re-run clears these and cannot clear those.
TRANSPORT_CODES = ("lane_unavailable", "lane_lost_midrun")
LANE_CHANNEL = "_lane"
DEFAULT_SOURCE_ROOT = Path.home() / ".claude" / "projects"
DEFAULT_DATA_DIR = Path.home() / ".hermes" / "mnemosyne" / "data"
HERMES_BANK = "default"
NEAR_DUPLICATE_COSINE = 0.92

# `- <name> [<annotation>] - <description> (added <date>)`; bin/fm-project-mode.sh
# owns what is inside the annotation, and is asked for the lane rather than parsed.
PROJECT_LINE_RE = re.compile(r"^-\s+(\S+)\s*(\[[^\]]*\])?\s*-?\s*(.*)$")

# How an auto-memory file's declared type becomes a stored memory. Report
# section 5.1 fixes the memory_type; section 3.5 fixes what importance and
# veracity mean. These files are the captain's own curated statements, so they
# are 'stated' - measured, dated fleet evidence is what 'tool' is for.
TYPE_MAP: Dict[str, Tuple[str, float, str]] = {
    "project": ("instruction", 0.7, "stated"),
    "feedback": ("preference", 0.85, "stated"),
    "user": ("preference", 0.85, "stated"),
    "reference": ("fact", 0.6, "stated"),
}
UNTYPED = ("context", 0.3, "unknown")


class MigrateError(Exception):
    """A refusal that must never be reported as a successful run."""


# --- credential detection ----------------------------------------------------
#
# Two independent rule classes, either of which keeps a file out of the store.
# They are separate so a later captain decision can loosen one without touching
# the other, and so every flag can say which one fired.

# A literal that is a secret on sight, wherever it appears. The last two carry
# a second test, because their shape alone also fits ordinary text: a long run
# of letters is usually a path or an identifier, not a key.
SECRET_VALUE_RULES: Sequence[Tuple[str, str, Optional[str]]] = (
    ("private_key_block", r"-----BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----", None),
    ("github_token", r"\bgh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]{16,}\b|\bgithub_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}\b", None),
    ("aws_access_key", r"\bAKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}\b", None),
    ("openai_style_key", r"\bsk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}\b", None),
    ("slack_token", r"\bxox[baprs]-[A-Za-z0-9-]{10,}\b", None),
    ("jwt", r"\beyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\.[A-Za-z0-9_-]{8,}\b", None),
    ("vendor_token", r"\bmpat_[A-Za-z0-9]{8,}\b", None),
    ("apple_auth_key_file", r"\bAuthKey_[A-Z0-9]{6,}\b", None),
    ("hex_digest_or_key", r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9])[0-9a-f]{32,}(?![A-Za-z0-9])", None),
    ("high_entropy_literal",
     r"(?<![A-Za-z0-9+/=])[A-Za-z0-9+/]{24,}={0,2}(?![A-Za-z0-9+/=])", "high_entropy"),
)
MIN_SECRET_ENTROPY_BITS = 3.6


def shannon_bits(text: str) -> float:
    """Bits of entropy per character - how unlike written language a run is."""
    if not text:
        return 0.0
    counts: Dict[str, int] = {}
    for char in text:
        counts[char] = counts.get(char, 0) + 1
    total = float(len(text))
    return -sum((n / total) * math.log2(n / total) for n in counts.values())


def high_entropy(token: str) -> bool:
    """A run of characters that no one wrote by hand.

    A path, a camelCase identifier, and a long word all match the shape of a
    base64 key, so shape alone is not enough. A generated secret mixes cases
    and digits AND spreads its characters far more evenly than any of them,
    and requiring both is what separates the two without a word list.
    """
    core = token.rstrip("=")
    if len(core) < 24:
        return False
    if not (any(c.isupper() for c in core) and any(c.islower() for c in core)
            and any(c.isdigit() for c in core)):
        return False
    return shannon_bits(core) >= MIN_SECRET_ENTROPY_BITS


SECRET_VALUE_VALIDATORS = {"high_entropy": high_entropy}

# Vocabulary that names a credential. On its own this is prose; next to a
# literal-shaped value it is a credential written down.
CREDENTIAL_TERMS = (
    "password", "passwd", "passphrase", "secret", "token", "api key", "api_key",
    "api-key", "apikey", "credential", "private key", "auth key", "authkey",
    "access key", "access-token", "access_token", "client secret", "bearer",
    "keychain", "issuer id", "team id", "key id", "keystore", "service account",
    "oauth", "app-specific password", "app specific password",
)
# Delimited on both sides, so `linkWithCredential` and "chibi tokens" are the
# ordinary prose they are rather than a credential this file writes down.
TERM_RE = re.compile(
    "(?<![A-Za-z0-9])(?:" + "|".join(re.escape(t) for t in CREDENTIAL_TERMS) + ")(?![A-Za-z0-9])",
    re.IGNORECASE)
ADJACENCY_CHARS = 80
# A run that looks like a written-down value rather than a word: it carries a
# digit, is an address, or is an all-caps identifier.
LITERAL_RE = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._@+/=:-]{5,}")

# A file that says on its face that it holds credentials, whatever its body
# happens to contain today. Matched against the file name and the declared
# title, as separated words, so 'automation' never reads as 'auth'.
DECLARED_CREDENTIAL_WORDS = frozenset(
    """credential credentials secret secrets password passwords passphrase token
    tokens auth apikey pat keychain privatekey signing""".split()
)


def looks_like_value(text: str) -> Optional[str]:
    for match in LITERAL_RE.finditer(text):
        token = match.group(0)
        if "@" in token:
            return token
        if any(ch.isdigit() for ch in token) and any(ch.isalpha() for ch in token):
            return token
        if token.isupper() and len(token) >= 6:
            return token
    return None


def redact(token: str) -> str:
    """Enough of a literal to recognise it, never enough to use it."""
    if len(token) <= 4:
        return "*" * len(token)
    return f"{token[:2]}{'*' * (len(token) - 4)}{token[-2:]}"


def words_of(text: str) -> Iterable[str]:
    return (w for w in re.split(r"[^A-Za-z0-9]+", text.lower()) if w)


def credential_findings(path_name: str, title: str, body: str) -> List[Dict[str, str]]:
    """Every reason this file must stay out of the store, with its evidence.

    The declared rule reads the file name and title; the value and adjacency
    rules read the title AND the body, because a hand-written summary can name
    a secret exactly as easily as the text it summarises.
    """
    findings: List[Dict[str, str]] = []
    body = f"{title}\n{body}" if title else body
    declared = sorted(set(words_of(f"{path_name} {title}")) & DECLARED_CREDENTIAL_WORDS)
    for word in declared:
        findings.append({
            "rule": "declared_credential_file",
            "detail": f"the name or title says {word!r}",
            "evidence": word,
        })
    for name, pattern, validator in SECRET_VALUE_RULES:
        check_value = SECRET_VALUE_VALIDATORS.get(validator or "")
        for match in re.finditer(pattern, body):
            if check_value and not check_value(match.group(0)):
                continue
            findings.append({
                "rule": f"secret_value:{name}",
                "detail": "a literal that is a secret on sight",
                "evidence": redact(match.group(0)),
            })
            break
    for match in TERM_RE.finditer(body):
        start = max(0, match.start() - ADJACENCY_CHARS)
        window = body[start:match.end() + ADJACENCY_CHARS]
        # The term itself is prose; a value beside it is the credential.
        value = looks_like_value(window.replace(match.group(0), " "))
        if value:
            findings.append({
                "rule": "credential_term_beside_value",
                "detail": f"{match.group(0).lower()!r} sits beside a written-down value",
                "evidence": redact(value),
            })
            break
    return findings


# --- parsing -----------------------------------------------------------------


class Parsed(NamedTuple):
    name: str
    description: str
    declared_type: Optional[str]
    modified: Optional[str]
    origin_session: Optional[str]
    body: str


def parse_scalar(raw: str) -> str:
    """One YAML scalar, in the shape Claude Code actually writes them."""
    value = raw.strip()
    if len(value) >= 2 and value[0] == value[-1] and value[0] in "\"'":
        return value[1:-1]
    return value


def parse_memory_file(path: Path) -> Parsed:
    """Both frontmatter dialects, and a file that carries neither.

    The newer generation nests the type under `metadata:`; the older one puts
    it at the top level. Nothing else about them differs, so both are read into
    the same shape rather than branching downstream on which one it was.
    """
    text = path.read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace")
    front: Dict[str, str] = {}
    body = text
    if text.startswith("---\n"):
        end = text.find("\n---", 3)
        if end != -1:
            block = text[4:end]
            body = text[end + 4:]
            if body.startswith("\n"):
                body = body[1:]
            in_metadata = False
            for line in block.splitlines():
                if not line.strip():
                    continue
                indented = line[:1].isspace()
                stripped = line.strip()
                if ":" not in stripped:
                    continue
                key, _, raw = stripped.partition(":")
                key = key.strip().lower()
                if not indented:
                    in_metadata = key == "metadata"
                    if in_metadata:
                        continue
                    front[key] = parse_scalar(raw)
                elif in_metadata:
                    front[f"metadata.{key}"] = parse_scalar(raw)
    declared = front.get("metadata.type") or front.get("type")
    return Parsed(
        name=front.get("name") or path.stem,
        description=front.get("description", ""),
        declared_type=(declared or None),
        modified=front.get("metadata.modified") or front.get("modified") or None,
        origin_session=front.get("metadata.originsessionid") or front.get("originsessionid") or None,
        body=body.strip(),
    )


def normalize(text: str) -> str:
    """The text two copies of one memory have in common.

    Case and whitespace are the only differences a hand-copied duplicate
    reliably picks up, so they are the only ones folded away. Anything more
    aggressive would start merging memories that genuinely differ.
    """
    return re.sub(r"\s+", " ", text).strip().lower()


def group_key(text: str) -> str:
    """The digest two hand-copied duplicates of one memory share.

    Folded, and for grouping only: it answers "are these the same fact
    written twice", never "does the store hold this" and never "is this the
    same memory as before".
    """
    return hashlib.sha256(normalize(text).encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


def identity_digest(text: str) -> str:
    """The digest that identifies a memory across time.

    The ledger asks whether the store already holds THIS memory, and the
    store's own dedupe compares content byte for byte. A folded digest would
    call a whitespace-only or case-only edit unchanged, skip the supersession,
    and leave the store holding two live rows for one file.
    """
    return hashlib.sha256(text.encode("utf-8")).hexdigest()


class StoredForm:
    """Text in the form the store actually holds it, and only ever that.

    Comparing derived text against store content directly is the mistake this
    type exists to make impossible: the store rewrites some content before it
    writes, so raw equality reads a correct migration as a lost memory. An
    equality against anything that is not a stored form RAISES rather than
    quietly answering False, because a quiet False is the failure this whole
    class of defect is made of.
    """

    __slots__ = ("text",)

    def __init__(self, text: str) -> None:
        self.text = text

    def __eq__(self, other: Any) -> bool:
        if isinstance(other, StoredForm):
            return self.text == other.text
        raise TypeError(
            "store content is only ever compared against another stored form; "
            f"got {type(other).__name__}. Put the other side through stored_form() "
            "so the store's own rewriting is modelled on both sides.")

    def __ne__(self, other: Any) -> bool:
        return not self.__eq__(other)

    def __hash__(self) -> int:
        return hash(("fm-memory-migrate.stored-form", self.text))

    def __repr__(self) -> str:
        return f"StoredForm({self.text[:60]!r})"


WIKILINK_RE = re.compile(r"\[\[([^\]]+)\]\]")

# Metadata whose values are lifted out of the file's body rather than its
# frontmatter, and which therefore carry whatever the body carries.
BODY_DERIVED_FIELDS = ("links",)


def wikilinks(body: str) -> List[str]:
    return sorted({m.group(1).strip() for m in WIKILINK_RE.finditer(body)})


# --- routing -----------------------------------------------------------------


def registered_projects(home: Path) -> List[str]:
    try:
        lines = (home / "data" / "projects.md").read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines()
    except OSError:
        return []
    out = []
    for line in lines:
        match = PROJECT_LINE_RE.match(line)
        if match and not line.startswith("- ["):
            out.append(match.group(1))
    return out


def slug_of(name: str) -> str:
    """A project name as it appears inside a Claude Code directory name."""
    return re.sub(r"[^A-Za-z0-9]+", "-", name)


def project_for_dir(dir_name: str, projects: Sequence[str]) -> Optional[str]:
    """The registered project a memory directory belongs to, or None.

    The directory name is the working directory with every non-alphanumeric run
    collapsed to '-', so it cannot be decoded back into a path: '-Coding-apps-
    tcg-ops' is both `Coding/apps/tcg-ops` and `Coding/apps-tcg-ops`. What it
    can do is END with the project's own name, and the LONGEST such name is the
    right one - '-Coding-suctrya-business-books' belongs to
    'suctrya-business-books', never to 'suctrya'. The boundary check is what
    keeps a name from matching the tail of a longer word.
    """
    best: Optional[str] = None
    for project in projects:
        suffix = slug_of(project)
        if not dir_name.endswith(suffix):
            continue
        head = dir_name[: -len(suffix)]
        if head and not head.endswith("-"):
            continue
        if best is None or len(suffix) > len(slug_of(best)):
            best = project
    return best


def project_lane(home: Path, project: str) -> Optional[str]:
    """The project's lane, from the script that owns the registry line format."""
    result = subprocess.run(  # noqa: S603
        [str(PROJECT_MODE), "--lane", project],
        env=dict(os.environ, FM_HOME=str(home)),
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    lane = result.stdout.strip()
    return lane if result.returncode == 0 and lane else None


# --- canonical-file collision ------------------------------------------------


def canonical_statements(home: Path) -> Dict[str, str]:
    """Normalized bodies of the sections bin/fm-memory-pointers already indexes.

    A memory whose text a canonical file states verbatim is that file's fact,
    and the pointer to it is the copy that stays correct when the file changes.
    """
    out: Dict[str, str] = {}
    for relative in ("data/captain.md", "data/learnings.md"):
        try:
            lines = (home / relative).read_text(encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").splitlines()
        except OSError:
            continue
        heading: Optional[str] = None
        body: List[str] = []
        for line in lines + ["## "]:
            if line.startswith("## "):
                if heading is not None and body:
                    out[normalize(" ".join(body))] = f"{relative} § {heading}"
                heading, body = line[3:].strip(), []
            elif heading is not None:
                body.append(line)
    return out


PARTIAL_OVERLAP_RATIO = 0.6
OVERLAP_WORD_RE = re.compile(r"[a-z0-9]{4,}")


def statement_words(text: str) -> set:
    return set(OVERLAP_WORD_RE.findall(normalize(text)))


def partial_overlap(body: str, statements: Dict[str, str]) -> Optional[Dict[str, Any]]:
    """How much of a memory a canonical section already says, short of all of it.

    An exact match is the pointer's fact and the memory is dropped. Anything
    less is reported and kept: dropping on a fuzzy signal loses whatever the
    memory says that the canonical file does not.
    """
    words = statement_words(body)
    if len(words) < 5:
        return None
    best: Optional[Dict[str, Any]] = None
    for statement, owner in statements.items():
        other = statement_words(statement)
        if not other:
            continue
        ratio = len(words & other) / len(words)
        if ratio >= PARTIAL_OVERLAP_RATIO and (best is None or ratio > best["overlap"]):
            best = {"owner": owner, "overlap": round(ratio, 2)}
    return best


# --- the migration plan ------------------------------------------------------


class SourceFile(NamedTuple):
    path: Path
    dir_name: str
    project: Optional[str]
    lane: Optional[str]
    parsed: Parsed
    findings: List[Dict[str, str]]


def scan_sources(source_root: Path, home: Path) -> Tuple[List[SourceFile], List[Dict[str, str]]]:
    projects = registered_projects(home)
    lanes: Dict[str, Optional[str]] = {}
    files: List[SourceFile] = []
    problems: List[Dict[str, str]] = []
    if not source_root.is_dir():
        raise MigrateError(f"no auto-memory source directory at {source_root}")
    for directory in sorted(source_root.iterdir()):
        memory_dir = directory / "memory"
        if not memory_dir.is_dir():
            continue
        project = project_for_dir(directory.name, projects)
        lane: Optional[str] = None
        if project is not None:
            if project not in lanes:
                lanes[project] = project_lane(home, project)
            lane = lanes[project]
        for path in sorted(memory_dir.glob("*.md")):
            if path.name == "MEMORY.md":
                continue
            try:
                parsed = parse_memory_file(path)
            except OSError as exc:
                problems.append({"path": str(path), "problem": f"unreadable: {exc}"})
                continue
            findings = credential_findings(path.name, f"{parsed.name} {parsed.description}", parsed.body)
            files.append(SourceFile(path, directory.name, project, lane, parsed, findings))
    return (files, problems)


class Entry(NamedTuple):
    key: str
    lane: str
    channel_id: str
    content: str
    memory_type: str
    importance: float
    veracity: str
    source: str
    metadata: Dict[str, Any]
    content_sha: str
    notes: List[str]


def entry_content(parsed: Parsed) -> str:
    """The stored text: the title first, so recall can match on it."""
    return f"[{parsed.name}] {parsed.body}".strip()


REDACTED_FIELD = "[redacted: this field is itself credential-bearing]"


def safe_field(text: str) -> Optional[str]:
    """A frontmatter field, but only if it is not itself secret-bearing.

    A title and a description are written by hand and can name a value as
    easily as the body can. Nothing derived from a credential-bearing file
    reaches the store without passing the same detection its body failed.
    """
    if not text or not text.strip():
        return None
    return text if not credential_findings("", "", text) else None


def credential_pointer_content(parsed: Parsed, paths: Sequence[str]) -> str:
    """A pointer that can be acted on and carries nothing that must not leave."""
    where = "; ".join(paths)
    title = safe_field(parsed.name) or "redacted title"
    text = (
        f"[{title}] Credential-bearing memory, kept OUT of the memory store by captain "
        f"decision D2 (2026-08-20): this store is served by a local dashboard, so its content is "
        f"never copied here. Read the file itself at {where}."
    )
    description = safe_field(parsed.description)
    if description:
        text += f" It covers: {description}"
    return text


def build_entries(files: Sequence[SourceFile], home: Path) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """Group, dedupe, classify, and route every source file."""
    routed = [f for f in files if f.lane]
    unrouted = [f for f in files if not f.lane]

    groups: Dict[str, List[SourceFile]] = {}
    for source in routed:
        groups.setdefault(group_key(source.parsed.body), []).append(source)

    statements = canonical_statements(home)
    entries: List[Entry] = []
    dropped: List[Dict[str, str]] = []
    overlaps: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
    cross_lane: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []

    for sha, members in sorted(groups.items(), key=lambda kv: kv[1][0].path.as_posix()):
        primary = members[0]
        owner = statements.get(normalize(primary.parsed.body))
        if owner:
            dropped.append({
                "name": primary.parsed.name,
                "paths": ", ".join(str(m.path) for m in members),
                "source_paths": [str(m.path) for m in members],
                "owner": owner,
            })
            continue
        overlap = partial_overlap(primary.parsed.body, statements)
        if overlap:
            overlaps.append({
                "name": primary.parsed.name,
                "paths": ", ".join(str(m.path) for m in members),
                **overlap,
            })
        by_lane: Dict[str, List[SourceFile]] = {}
        for member in members:
            by_lane.setdefault(str(member.lane), []).append(member)
        if len(by_lane) > 1:
            cross_lane.append({
                "name": primary.parsed.name,
                "lanes": sorted(by_lane),
                "paths": [str(m.path) for m in members],
            })
        for lane, lane_members in sorted(by_lane.items()):
            entries.append(make_entry(lane, lane_members, sha, len(by_lane) > 1))

    disambiguate(entries)
    return {
        "entries": entries,
        "dropped_for_pointer": dropped,
        "partial_pointer_overlaps": overlaps,
        "cross_lane_duplicates": cross_lane,
        "unrouted": unrouted,
        "groups": groups,
    }


def make_entry(lane: str, members: Sequence[SourceFile], sha: str, cross_lane: bool) -> Entry:
    primary = members[0]
    parsed = primary.parsed
    projects = sorted({str(m.project) for m in members})
    paths = [str(m.path) for m in members]
    # Copies of one fact under several projects of one lane are what the
    # lane-wide channel is for; that duplication is the reason lanes exist.
    channel = projects[0] if len(projects) == 1 else LANE_CHANNEL
    findings = [f for m in members for f in m.findings]
    notes: List[str] = []
    if len(members) > 1:
        notes.append(f"canonicalised {len(members)} copies")
    if channel == LANE_CHANNEL:
        notes.append(f"lane-wide across {', '.join(projects)}")
    if cross_lane:
        notes.append("also present in another lane")

    metadata: Dict[str, Any] = {
        "migration": MIGRATION_MARKER,
        "source": SOURCE,
        "lane": lane,
        "source_path": paths[0],
        "source_paths": paths,
        "source_dirs": sorted({m.dir_name for m in members}),
        "source_projects": projects,
        "source_name": parsed.name,
        "description": parsed.description,
        "content_sha": sha,
    }
    if parsed.declared_type:
        metadata["source_type"] = parsed.declared_type
    if parsed.modified:
        # The bridge exposes no way to set a row's timestamp, and reaching past
        # it into sqlite is the direct-store access this design refuses. The
        # file's own modified time is preserved here instead of being lost.
        metadata["source_modified"] = parsed.modified
    sessions = sorted({m.parsed.origin_session for m in members if m.parsed.origin_session})
    if sessions:
        metadata["origin_session_ids"] = sessions
    links = wikilinks(parsed.body)
    if links:
        metadata["links"] = links

    if findings:
        metadata["credential_pointer"] = True
        metadata["credential_findings"] = findings
        # The provenance fields are copied from the same file the body was
        # refused from, so they are held to the same test before they land.
        for field in ("description", "source_name"):
            if metadata.get(field) and safe_field(str(metadata[field])) is None:
                metadata[field] = REDACTED_FIELD
        # Text lifted out of the refused body is held to that test too: a
        # link target is body text, and D2 keeps the body out of the store.
        for field in BODY_DERIVED_FIELDS:
            kept = [v for v in metadata.get(field) or [] if safe_field(str(v))]
            if kept:
                metadata[field] = kept
            else:
                metadata.pop(field, None)
        content = credential_pointer_content(parsed, paths)
        notes.append("credential-bearing: pointer only, content not copied")
        return Entry(
            key=entry_key(lane, channel, primary, sha),
            lane=lane,
            channel_id=channel,
            content=content,
            memory_type="reference",
            importance=0.7,
            veracity="stated",
            source=POINTER_SOURCE,
            metadata=metadata,
            content_sha=identity_digest(content),
            notes=notes,
        )

    memory_type, importance, veracity = TYPE_MAP.get(parsed.declared_type or "", UNTYPED)
    content = entry_content(parsed)
    return Entry(
        key=entry_key(lane, channel, primary, sha),
        lane=lane,
        channel_id=channel,
        content=content,
        memory_type=memory_type,
        importance=importance,
        veracity=veracity,
        source=SOURCE,
        metadata=metadata,
        content_sha=identity_digest(content),
        notes=notes,
    )


def entry_key(lane: str, channel: str, primary: SourceFile, sha: str) -> str:
    """A ledger key stable across the fragmentation this tool collapses.

    Deliberately the memory's own file name under its resolved channel, not a
    source path: the same file under a second directory of the same project is
    the SAME memory, and keying by path would make it a second one the moment
    the captain worked from a different checkout.
    Two different memories can share a file name inside one channel - four
    EquiMe directories collapse into one channel - so the key carries a digest
    of the memory's own text. Nothing in it depends on which of its same-stem
    siblings happens to exist, because a key that shifted when a sibling went
    missing would point the supersession at another memory's row.
    """
    return f"{lane}/{channel}/{primary.path.stem}@{sha[:12]}"


def disambiguate(entries: List[Entry]) -> None:
    """Two memories whose short digests collide still need separate keys."""
    seen: Dict[str, int] = {}
    for entry in entries:
        seen[entry.key] = seen.get(entry.key, 0) + 1
    for index, entry in enumerate(entries):
        if seen[entry.key] > 1:
            entries[index] = entry._replace(
                key=f"{entry.key}@{entry.metadata['content_sha']}")


# --- the source manifest (mirror mode's proof) -------------------------------


def canonicalised_projects(files: Sequence[SourceFile]) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
    """Which projects were assembled from more than one memory directory."""
    by_project: Dict[str, List[SourceFile]] = {}
    for source in files:
        if source.project:
            by_project.setdefault(source.project, []).append(source)
    out = []
    for project, members in sorted(by_project.items()):
        directories = sorted({m.dir_name for m in members})
        if len(directories) > 1:
            out.append({
                "project": project,
                "lane": members[0].lane,
                "directories": directories,
                "files": len(members),
            })
    return out


def unrouted_fragments(files: Sequence[SourceFile]) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
    """Unrouted directories that look like one project split across paths.

    An observation, never a routing decision: these are named so the captain
    can register the project once instead of discovering the split later. The
    trailing-segment match has to be long enough that two unrelated
    directories ending in a short common word are not called one project.
    """
    minimum = 5
    directories = sorted({f.dir_name for f in files if not f.project})
    groups: Dict[str, List[str]] = {}
    for index, left in enumerate(directories):
        for right in directories[index + 1:]:
            suffix = common_suffix_segments(left, right)
            if len(suffix) >= minimum:
                groups.setdefault(suffix, [])
                for name in (left, right):
                    if name not in groups[suffix]:
                        groups[suffix].append(name)
    out = []
    for suffix, names in sorted(groups.items()):
        out.append({
            "looks_like_project": suffix,
            "directories": sorted(names),
            "files": len([f for f in files if f.dir_name in names]),
        })
    return out


def common_suffix_segments(left: str, right: str) -> str:
    """The longest trailing run of '-' separated segments the two share."""
    a, b = left.split("-"), right.split("-")
    shared: List[str] = []
    for x, y in zip(reversed(a), reversed(b)):
        if x != y:
            break
        shared.insert(0, x)
    return "-".join(shared)


def source_manifest(files: Sequence[SourceFile]) -> Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]]:
    out: Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]] = {}
    for source in files:
        try:
            raw = source.path.read_bytes()
        except OSError as exc:
            out[str(source.path)] = {"error": str(exc)}
            continue
        out[str(source.path)] = {
            "bytes": len(raw),
            "sha256": hashlib.sha256(raw).hexdigest(),
        }
    return out


def manifest_drift(before: Dict[str, Any], after: Dict[str, Any]) -> List[str]:
    drift = []
    for path in sorted(set(before) | set(after)):
        if before.get(path) != after.get(path):
            drift.append(path)
    return drift


def hermes_record_path(data_dir: Path) -> Path:
    return data_dir / "fm-memory-migrate-hermes.json"


def read_hermes_record(data_dir: Path) -> Optional[Dict[str, Any]]:
    try:
        record = json.loads(hermes_record_path(data_dir).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    return record if isinstance(record, dict) else None


def write_hermes_record(data_dir: Path, before: Dict[str, Any], after: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:
    """What Hermes's own bank looked like on either side of a migration run.

    Hermes keeps writing to that bank in normal operation, so 'unchanged since
    the migration' can only be judged across the run itself. Recording both
    sides is what lets a later `verify` state that fact instead of comparing
    against a number that has moved for legitimate reasons.
    """
    try:
        hermes_record_path(data_dir).write_text(
            json.dumps({"before": before, "after": after}, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n",
            encoding="utf-8")
    except OSError:
        pass


def hermes_intrusion(data_dir: Path) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """Whether anything this migration writes has landed in Hermes's own bank.

    The property that matters is not that the bank is byte-identical - Hermes
    writes to it constantly and no before/after comparison over concurrently
    written state can establish anything - but that no row in it carries this
    migration's provenance. That is what a stray write from here would look
    like, and Hermes's own activity cannot fake it either way.
    """
    db = data_dir / "banks" / HERMES_BANK / "mnemosyne.db"
    if not db.is_file():
        return {"present": False, "rows": 0}
    out: Dict[str, Any] = {"present": True, "rows": 0}
    try:
        conn = sqlite3.connect(f"file:{db}?mode=ro", uri=True)
    except sqlite3.Error as exc:
        out["error"] = str(exc)
        return out
    placeholders = ", ".join("?" for _ in MIGRATED_SOURCES)
    found: List[str] = []
    unreadable: List[str] = []
    try:
        # A memory the store consolidated out of working_memory lives on in
        # episodic_memory, so both are read: a stray write of ours must not be
        # able to hide by being moved between them. A table this bank does not
        # have holds nothing and is not an unproven claim.
        for table in ("working_memory", "episodic_memory"):
            try:
                present = conn.execute(
                    "SELECT 1 FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table' AND name=?", (table,)).fetchone()
                if not present:
                    continue
                rows = conn.execute(
                    f"SELECT id FROM {table} WHERE source IN ({placeholders}) "  # noqa: S608
                    f"OR metadata_json LIKE ?",
                    (*MIGRATED_SOURCES, f'%"{MIGRATION_MARKER}"%'),
                ).fetchall()
            except sqlite3.Error as exc:
                unreadable.append(f"{table}: {exc}")
                continue
            found.extend(str(row[0]) for row in rows)
    finally:
        conn.close()
    out["rows"] = len(found)
    out["ids"] = found[:5]
    if unreadable:
        out["error"] = "; ".join(unreadable)
    return out


def hermes_snapshot(data_dir: Path) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """Informational only: what Hermes's bank looked like at one instant.

    Never a pass/fail signal. Hermes mutates its own mtime and row counts for
    reasons that have nothing to do with this migration.
    """
    db = data_dir / "banks" / HERMES_BANK / "mnemosyne.db"
    if not db.is_file():
        return {"present": False}
    out: Dict[str, Any] = {"present": True, "mtime_ns": db.stat().st_mtime_ns}
    try:
        conn = sqlite3.connect(f"file:{db}?mode=ro", uri=True)
    except sqlite3.Error as exc:
        out["error"] = str(exc)
        return out
    try:
        for table in ("working_memory", "episodic_memory"):
            out[table] = conn.execute(f"SELECT count(*) FROM {table}").fetchone()[0]  # noqa: S608
    except sqlite3.Error as exc:
        out["error"] = str(exc)
    finally:
        conn.close()
    return out


# --- the ledger --------------------------------------------------------------


def ledger_path(data_dir: Path, lane: str) -> Path:
    return data_dir / "banks" / f"lane-{lane}" / LEDGER_FILENAME


def read_ledger(data_dir: Path, lane: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    try:
        raw = json.loads(ledger_path(data_dir, lane).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    section = raw.get("memories") if isinstance(raw, dict) else None
    return section if isinstance(section, dict) else {}


def write_ledger(data_dir: Path, lane: str, records: Dict[str, Any]) -> None:
    path = ledger_path(data_dir, lane)
    try:
        path.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        path.write_text(
            json.dumps({"version": 1, "memories": records},
                       indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n",
            encoding="utf-8",
        )
    except OSError:
        # A ledger that cannot be written costs one duplicate on a later edit,
        # never a wrong answer, so it must not lose the writes that did land.
        pass


def refusals_path(data_dir: Path) -> Path:
    return data_dir / REFUSALS_FILENAME


def read_refusals(data_dir: Path, lane: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """What the last run asked this lane's store for and did not get.

    A refusal is recorded rather than inferred. Nothing the store leaves
    behind distinguishes a write it rejected from a source edited since the
    last run, and telling a captain to re-run a write that will be refused
    again is worse than saying nothing.
    It lives beside the data dir rather than inside a bank, because the lane
    that most needs a refusal recorded is the one whose bank would not open,
    and writing a per-bank file for it would mint the bank directory as a side
    effect of the failure it is reporting.
    """
    try:
        raw = json.loads(refusals_path(data_dir).read_text(encoding="utf-8"))
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return {}
    lanes = raw.get("lanes") if isinstance(raw, dict) else None
    section = lanes.get(lane) if isinstance(lanes, dict) else None
    return section if isinstance(section, dict) else {}


def write_refusals(data_dir: Path, by_lane: Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]]) -> None:
    """Replace the recorded refusals with what this run actually got told.

    Written whole rather than merged, so a lane that ran clean stops carrying
    the refusal of a run that no longer describes it.
    """
    lanes = {lane: records for lane, records in by_lane.items() if records}
    try:
        data_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        refusals_path(data_dir).write_text(
            json.dumps({"version": 1, "lanes": lanes}, indent=2, sort_keys=True) + "\n",
            encoding="utf-8",
        )
    except OSError:
        pass


# --- the bridge --------------------------------------------------------------


class BridgeSession:
    """One `fm-memory-mcp serve` process for one lane, over its own stdio."""

    def __init__(self, lane: str, data_dir: Path, home: Path) -> None:
        self.lane = lane
        self._next_id = 1
        self.proc = subprocess.Popen(  # noqa: S603
            [sys.executable, str(BRIDGE), "serve", "--lane", lane,
             "--data-dir", str(data_dir), "--session", SESSION],
            stdin=subprocess.PIPE,
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
            stderr=subprocess.PIPE,
            env=dict(os.environ, FM_HOME=str(home)),
            text=True,
            bufsize=1,
        )
        try:
            self._call_raw("initialize", {})
        except BaseException:
            self.close()
            raise

    def _call_raw(self, method: str, params: Dict[str, Any]) -> Dict[str, Any]:
        assert self.proc.stdin and self.proc.stdout
        request = {"jsonrpc": "2.0", "id": self._next_id, "method": method, "params": params}
        self._next_id += 1
        try:
            self.proc.stdin.write(json.dumps(request) + "\n")
            self.proc.stdin.flush()
            line = self.proc.stdout.readline()
        except (OSError, ValueError) as exc:
            raise MigrateError(f"lane {self.lane}: the bridge closed its pipe: {exc}") from exc
        if not line:
            try:
                stderr = (self.proc.stderr.read() if self.proc.stderr else "").strip()
            except (OSError, ValueError):
                stderr = ""
            raise MigrateError(f"lane {self.lane}: the bridge answered nothing. {stderr}")
        try:
            return json.loads(line)
        except ValueError as exc:
            raise MigrateError(f"lane {self.lane}: the bridge answered unparseable JSON: {exc}") from exc

    def call_tool(self, name: str, arguments: Dict[str, Any]) -> Tuple[bool, Dict[str, Any]]:
        """(ok, payload) - a typed refusal is data, never an exception."""
        response = self._call_raw("tools/call", {"name": name, "arguments": arguments})
        if "result" not in response:
            raise MigrateError(f"lane {self.lane}: {json.dumps(response.get('error'))}")
        result = response["result"]
        try:
            payload = json.loads(result["content"][0]["text"])
        except (ValueError, KeyError, IndexError, TypeError) as exc:
            raise MigrateError(f"lane {self.lane}: unreadable {name} result: {exc}") from exc
        if not isinstance(payload, dict):
            raise MigrateError(
                f"lane {self.lane}: the bridge answered {name} with "
                f"{type(payload).__name__}, not an object")
        return (not result.get("isError", False), payload)

    def close(self) -> None:
        """Tear the bridge down. Never raises: a dead bridge costs its lane."""
        try:
            self._call_raw("shutdown", {})
        except (MigrateError, OSError, ValueError):
            pass
        finally:
            for stream in (self.proc.stdin, self.proc.stdout, self.proc.stderr):
                if stream is not None:
                    try:
                        stream.close()
                    except OSError:
                        pass
            try:
                self.proc.wait(timeout=30)
            except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
                self.proc.kill()
                try:
                    self.proc.wait(timeout=30)
                except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError):
                    pass
            except OSError:
                pass


def same_memory(record: Any, entry: Entry) -> bool:
    """Whether a ledger record and a derived entry are the same memory.

    A source file moves along two axes and identity has to survive both. An
    edit keeps the file's paths and changes its text; a rename keeps its text
    and changes its paths. Either match is the same memory, and the union is
    what makes this stable: matching on paths alone calls a rename a new
    memory, matching on text alone calls an edit one.
    A file renamed AND edited in one interval matches on neither, and is
    genuinely indistinguishable from that file being deleted and another
    created. It is treated as exactly that, which under the never-retire rule
    means the old record is reported as a kept orphan and the new file becomes
    a new memory. That bound is stated in the header, not guessed at here.
    """
    record = record or {}
    if set(entry.metadata.get("source_paths") or []) & set(record.get("source_paths") or []):
        return True
    return bool(record.get("content_sha")) and record["content_sha"] == entry.content_sha


def sources_readable(entry: Entry) -> bool:
    """Whether every file this memory is derived from is on disk right now.

    The never-retire rule exists because a missing file is as likely to be a
    rename or an unmounted directory as a deletion. Nothing that supersedes a
    memory may run on that uncertainty, so a re-placement is only ever offered
    when every source this memory came from can be read this instant.
    """
    paths = entry.metadata.get("source_paths") or []
    if not paths:
        return False
    for path in paths:
        try:
            with open(path, "rb"):
                pass
        except OSError:
            return False
    return True


def ledger_record_for(ledger: Dict[str, Any], entry: Entry) -> Tuple[Optional[str], Dict[str, Any]]:
    """(key, record) for the row this entry's memory landed in.

    Identity is what the record carries, not the key it happens to sit under,
    and every caller asks this one question here. A record that is not this
    memory belongs to another one, and superseding it would retire a live
    memory this run never derived - exactly what the never-retire rule forbids.
    A record found under a different key is this memory re-keyed: its file was
    renamed, or the digest in its key moved because its text changed.
    """
    record = ledger.get(entry.key)
    if record is not None and same_memory(record, entry):
        return (entry.key, dict(record))
    for key in sorted(ledger):
        if key != entry.key and same_memory(ledger[key], entry):
            return (key, dict(ledger[key]))
    return (None, {})


def write_arguments(entry: Entry) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    args: Dict[str, Any] = {
        "content": entry.content,
        "memory_type": entry.memory_type,
        "importance": entry.importance,
        "veracity": entry.veracity,
        "scope": "global",
        "source": entry.source,
        "metadata": entry.metadata,
    }
    if entry.channel_id != LANE_CHANNEL:
        args["project"] = entry.channel_id
    return args


def record_floor(data_dir: Path, lane: str, home: Path) -> Optional[str]:
    """Re-record the lane's row floor, so preflight measures against its new size.

    The floor is what tells a wiped bank from a quiet one. Left at the
    pre-migration size it would still pass while most of the migration was
    missing, which is the exact success-shaped emptiness this bridge exists to
    make impossible.
    """
    result = subprocess.run(  # noqa: S603
        [sys.executable, str(BRIDGE), "record-floor", "--lane", lane, "--data-dir", str(data_dir)],
        env=dict(os.environ, FM_HOME=str(home)),
        capture_output=True,
        text=True,
        check=False,
    )
    if result.returncode == 0:
        return None
    return (result.stderr.strip() or result.stdout.strip() or "record-floor failed")


def write_migration(entries: Sequence[Entry], data_dir: Path, home: Path) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    by_lane: Dict[str, List[Entry]] = {}
    for entry in entries:
        by_lane.setdefault(entry.lane, []).append(entry)

    counts = {"written": 0, "skipped_duplicate": 0, "updated": 0, "refused": 0}
    refusals: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
    # What this run asked for and did not get, per lane, recorded as fact so
    # `verify` reads it rather than guessing from what landed.
    refused_records: Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]] = {}
    lane_reports: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
    orphaned: List[Dict[str, str]] = []

    for lane in sorted(by_lane):
        lane_entries = by_lane[lane]
        ledger = read_ledger(data_dir, lane)
        try:
            session = BridgeSession(lane, data_dir, home)
        except (MigrateError, OSError) as exc:
            counts["refused"] += len(lane_entries)
            unavailable: Dict[str, Any] = {}
            for entry in lane_entries:
                refusals.append({"key": entry.key, "lane": lane, "code": "lane_unavailable", "message": str(exc)})
                unavailable[entry.key] = {
                    "content_sha": entry.content_sha,
                    "code": "lane_unavailable",
                    "message": str(exc),
                    "source_paths": entry.metadata["source_paths"],
                }
            refused_records[lane] = unavailable
            lane_reports.append({"lane": lane, "state": "unavailable", "detail": str(exc)})
            continue

        lane_counts = {"written": 0, "skipped_duplicate": 0, "updated": 0, "refused": 0}
        lane_state = "ready"
        lane_detail = ""
        lane_refused: Dict[str, Any] = {}
        try:
            for index, entry in enumerate(lane_entries):
                try:
                    previous_key, record = ledger_record_for(ledger, entry)
                    previous_id = record.get("memory_id")
                    # Only a memory whose own files are readable right now is
                    # ever superseded. A source that has vanished stays kept and
                    # reported, which is the never-retire rule, and nothing here
                    # may act on that uncertainty.
                    changed = (bool(previous_id)
                               and record.get("content_sha") != entry.content_sha
                               and sources_readable(entry))
                    ok = False
                    payload: Dict[str, Any] = {}
                    if changed:
                        ok, payload = session.call_tool(
                            "memory_supersede", dict(write_arguments(entry), memory_id=previous_id))
                        if ok:
                            lane_counts["updated"] += 1
                    if not ok:
                        # The ledger is derived, so a supersession it asked for that
                        # the store refused - the row is gone, or was already
                        # retired - falls back to writing the memory plainly rather
                        # than losing it to a record that no longer describes reality.
                        ok, payload = session.call_tool("memory_remember", write_arguments(entry))
                        if ok:
                            if payload.get("created"):
                                lane_counts["written"] += 1
                            else:
                                lane_counts["skipped_duplicate"] += 1
                    if not ok:
                        lane_counts["refused"] += 1
                        error = payload.get("error") or {}
                        refusals.append({
                            "key": entry.key,
                            "lane": lane,
                            "code": error.get("code", "unknown"),
                            "message": error.get("message", json.dumps(payload)),
                        })
                        lane_refused[entry.key] = {
                            "content_sha": entry.content_sha,
                            "code": error.get("code", "unknown"),
                            "message": error.get("message", json.dumps(payload)),
                            "source_paths": entry.metadata["source_paths"],
                        }
                        continue
                    memory_id = payload.get("id")
                    if memory_id:
                        if previous_key and previous_key != entry.key:
                            ledger.pop(previous_key, None)
                        ledger[entry.key] = {
                            "memory_id": memory_id,
                            "content_sha": entry.content_sha,
                            "source_paths": entry.metadata["source_paths"],
                        }
                except (MigrateError, OSError) as exc:
                    # A bridge that dies mid-lane costs that lane's remaining
                    # memories and no others. The writes that did land keep their
                    # ledger entries and every other lane is still attempted,
                    # because stopping the run here would leave how much of the
                    # fleet is indexed unknowable.
                    lane_state, lane_detail = "lost", str(exc)
                    remaining = lane_entries[index:]
                    lane_counts["refused"] += len(remaining)
                    for lost in remaining:
                        refusals.append({
                            "key": lost.key, "lane": lane,
                            "code": "lane_lost_midrun", "message": str(exc),
                        })
                        lane_refused[lost.key] = {
                            "content_sha": lost.content_sha,
                            "code": "lane_lost_midrun",
                            "message": str(exc),
                            "source_paths": lost.metadata["source_paths"],
                        }
                    break
        finally:
            session.close()

        orphaned.extend(orphaned_ledger_keys(lane_entries, [lane], {lane: ledger}))
        write_ledger(data_dir, lane, ledger)
        refused_records[lane] = lane_refused

        floor_error = None
        if lane_counts["written"] or lane_counts["updated"]:
            floor_error = record_floor(data_dir, lane, home)
        report = {"lane": lane, "state": lane_state, **lane_counts}
        if lane_detail:
            report["detail"] = lane_detail
        if floor_error:
            report["row_floor_error"] = floor_error
        lane_reports.append(report)
        for key in lane_counts:
            counts[key] += lane_counts[key]

    # A lane this run derived nothing for still has a ledger, and its keys are
    # exactly the ones most likely to have lost their source. Walking only the
    # lanes with work would leave that invisible, which is the one thing a
    # deliberately non-retiring tool must not do.
    for lane in sorted(ledger_lanes(data_dir)):
        if lane in by_lane:
            continue
        for key, record in sorted(read_ledger(data_dir, lane).items()):
            orphaned.append({"lane": lane, "key": key, "memory_id": (record or {}).get("memory_id", "")})

    write_refusals(data_dir, refused_records)

    return {
        "counts": counts,
        "lanes": lane_reports,
        "refusals": refusals,
        "orphaned_ledger_keys": orphaned,
    }


def ledger_lanes(data_dir: Path) -> List[str]:
    """Every lane whose bank carries a ledger this tool wrote."""
    banks = data_dir / "banks"
    if not banks.is_dir():
        return []
    out = []
    for path in sorted(banks.iterdir()):
        if path.name.startswith("lane-") and (path / LEDGER_FILENAME).is_file():
            out.append(path.name[len("lane-"):])
    return out


# --- verification (report section 5.5) ---------------------------------------


MIGRATED_SOURCES = (SOURCE, POINTER_SOURCE)


def open_bank(data_dir: Path, lane: str) -> Optional[sqlite3.Connection]:
    db = data_dir / "banks" / f"lane-{lane}" / "mnemosyne.db"
    if not db.is_file():
        return None
    try:
        return sqlite3.connect(f"file:{db}?mode=ro", uri=True)
    except sqlite3.Error:
        return None


def count_indexed(conn: sqlite3.Connection, table: str, column: str,
                  ids: Sequence[str]) -> Optional[int]:
    """How many of these memory ids the given index table holds."""
    found = 0
    for start in range(0, len(ids), 400):
        chunk = list(ids[start:start + 400])
        placeholders = ", ".join("?" for _ in chunk)
        try:
            row = conn.execute(
                f"SELECT count(*) FROM {table} WHERE {column} IN ({placeholders})",  # noqa: S608
                chunk).fetchone()
        except sqlite3.Error:
            return None
        found += row[0]
    return found


def bank_channels(conn: sqlite3.Connection) -> List[str]:
    """Every channel this bank holds a memory under, migrated or not.

    A memory that leaked into the wrong lane carries its own project channel,
    and recall only searches the channels it is asked for, so the probe has to
    ask the bank which channels exist rather than assume the lane-wide one.
    """
    try:
        rows = conn.execute("SELECT DISTINCT channel_id FROM working_memory").fetchall()
    except sqlite3.Error:
        return []
    return sorted({row[0] for row in rows if row[0]})


def is_retired(row: Dict[str, Any]) -> bool:
    """Superseded, or already past its shelf life.

    The store's own recall filter is `valid_until IS NULL OR valid_until >
    now`, compared as text against a local ISO timestamp, so this reads it the
    same way. An expiry still in the future has not happened yet, and calling
    such a row retired would report a live memory as lost.
    """
    if row["superseded_by"]:
        return True
    valid_until = row["valid_until"]
    return bool(valid_until) and str(valid_until) <= datetime.now().isoformat()


def migrated_rows(conn: sqlite3.Connection) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
    placeholders = ", ".join("?" for _ in MIGRATED_SOURCES)
    rows = conn.execute(
        f"SELECT id, content, source, scope, channel_id, consolidated_at, superseded_by, "  # noqa: S608
        f"valid_until, metadata_json, importance FROM working_memory WHERE source IN ({placeholders})",
        MIGRATED_SOURCES,
    ).fetchall()
    out = []
    for row in rows:
        try:
            metadata = json.loads(row[8]) if row[8] else {}
        except ValueError:
            metadata = {}
        out.append({
            "id": row[0], "content": row[1], "source": row[2], "scope": row[3],
            "channel_id": row[4], "consolidated_at": row[5], "superseded_by": row[6],
            "valid_until": row[7], "metadata": metadata, "importance": row[9],
        })
    return out


def cosine(a: Sequence[float], b: Sequence[float]) -> float:
    dot = sum(x * y for x, y in zip(a, b))
    na = math.sqrt(sum(x * x for x in a))
    nb = math.sqrt(sum(y * y for y in b))
    if na == 0 or nb == 0:
        return 0.0
    return dot / (na * nb)


def near_duplicates(conn: sqlite3.Connection, ids: Sequence[str]) -> List[Dict[str, Any]]:
    """Pairs the store's own embeddings put at or above the review threshold.

    Computed from what the store wrote rather than from a second embedding
    model, so this cannot disagree with what recall will actually rank
    together. Reported for review and never merged.
    """
    vectors: Dict[str, List[float]] = {}
    wanted = set(ids)
    try:
        rows = conn.execute("SELECT memory_id, embedding_json FROM memory_embeddings").fetchall()
    except sqlite3.Error:
        return []
    for memory_id, raw in rows:
        if memory_id not in wanted:
            continue
        try:
            vector = json.loads(raw)
        except ValueError:
            continue
        if isinstance(vector, list) and vector:
            vectors[memory_id] = vector
    out = []
    keys = sorted(vectors)
    for i, left in enumerate(keys):
        for right in keys[i + 1:]:
            score = cosine(vectors[left], vectors[right])
            if score >= NEAR_DUPLICATE_COSINE:
                out.append({"a": left, "b": right, "cosine": round(score, 4)})
    return out


def orphaned_ledger_keys(entries: Sequence[Entry], lanes: Sequence[str],
                        ledgers: Dict[str, Dict[str, Any]]) -> List[Dict[str, str]]:
    """Ledger keys this run derives nothing for: their source file is gone.

    They are reported, never retired: under mirror mode a missing file is as
    likely to be a rename or an unmounted directory as a deletion.
    Which record belongs to a memory is the same question `write` asks, and it
    is answered the same way: by the source files the record carries. A key
    this run derives nothing for is not an orphan when its record holds a file
    this run still derives - an edited file re-keys, because the key carries a
    digest of the memory's text - nor when the row it names is one a derived
    entry already claims, which is what a rename leaves behind. What is left
    is a record whose source files this run no longer sees at all.
    """
    out: List[Dict[str, str]] = []
    for lane in lanes:
        ledger = ledgers.get(lane) or {}
        claimed_keys = set()
        claimed_ids = set()
        for entry in entries:
            if entry.lane != lane:
                continue
            key, record = ledger_record_for(ledger, entry)
            claimed_keys.add(entry.key)
            if key:
                claimed_keys.add(key)
            if record.get("memory_id"):
                claimed_ids.add(record["memory_id"])
        for key in sorted(set(ledger) - claimed_keys):
            record = ledger[key] or {}
            if record.get("memory_id") in claimed_ids:
                continue
            out.append({"lane": lane, "key": key, "memory_id": record.get("memory_id", "")})
    return out


@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def stored_form(text: str) -> StoredForm:
    """The text the store would hold for this content.

    The library rewrites a data: URI, anything over 1 MB, and high-entropy
    content over 100 KB into a content-addressed stub before it writes, so a
    row can legitimately hold something other than what it was handed. This is
    the same sanitizer the bridge keys its own dedupe on, imported rather than
    restated so the two can never drift apart.
    Extraction writes the bytes it lifts out to a blob directory. `verify`
    only reads, so that write is pointed at a directory thrown away here: the
    stub is derived from the content's own digest and reads the same either
    way.
    A sanitizer that will not load raises rather than falling back to the raw
    text, the way the bridge refuses `library_unavailable` rather than writing
    unsanitized: comparing raw text against a bank written with sanitization
    would report every rewritten memory as never landed, and answering a
    question this cannot answer is worse than saying it cannot.
    """
    try:
        from mnemosyne.core.content_sanitizer import sanitize_content  # noqa: PLC0415
    except ImportError as exc:
        raise MigrateError(
            "the store's sanitizer could not be loaded "
            f"(mnemosyne.core.content_sanitizer: {exc}), so what the bank holds "
            "cannot be compared") from exc
    previous = os.environ.get("MNEMOSYNE_BLOB_DIR")
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="fm-memory-migrate-verify-") as scratch:
        os.environ["MNEMOSYNE_BLOB_DIR"] = scratch
        try:
            sanitized, _blob = sanitize_content(text)
        except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001 - a sanitizer that cannot run proves nothing
            raise MigrateError(
                "the store's sanitizer could not be run "
                f"(mnemosyne.core.content_sanitizer: {exc}), so what the bank "
                "holds cannot be compared") from exc
        finally:
            if previous is None:
                os.environ.pop("MNEMOSYNE_BLOB_DIR", None)
            else:
                os.environ["MNEMOSYNE_BLOB_DIR"] = previous
    return StoredForm(sanitized)


def holds(row: Dict[str, Any], entry: Entry) -> bool:
    """Whether this store row holds this entry's current text.

    The one answer to materialisation, and never asked with identity: a row
    that carries the id the ledger recorded proves only that something once
    landed for this memory, which is exactly what a refused update leaves
    behind. Both sides go through the store's own form, so the rewriting it
    applies is modelled on each of them.
    """
    return stored_form(row.get("content") or "") == stored_form(entry.content)


def refused_for(refusals: Dict[str, Any], entry: Entry) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """What the store refused for this exact memory, as the last run recorded it."""
    record = refusals.get(entry.key)
    if isinstance(record, dict) and record.get("content_sha") == entry.content_sha:
        return record
    paths = set(entry.metadata.get("source_paths") or [])
    for candidate in refusals.values():
        if not isinstance(candidate, dict):
            continue
        if candidate.get("content_sha") != entry.content_sha:
            continue
        if paths & set(candidate.get("source_paths") or []):
            return candidate
    return {}


def check(name: str, passed: bool, detail: str) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    return {"check": name, "pass": passed, "detail": detail}


def verify(entries: Sequence[Entry], files: Sequence[SourceFile], data_dir: Path,
           home: Path, dropped: Sequence[Dict[str, Any]] = (),
           problems: Sequence[Dict[str, str]] = ()) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    """The integrity checklist, read from the banks rather than from a report."""
    checks: List[Dict[str, Any]] = []
    # The ledgers name lanes this run derived nothing for, and their keys are
    # exactly the memories whose source file is gone. verify has to read them
    # the way write does or it cannot tell a kept orphan from a lost memory.
    lanes = sorted({entry.lane for entry in entries} | set(ledger_lanes(data_dir)))
    rows_by_lane: Dict[str, List[Dict[str, Any]]] = {}
    channels_by_lane: Dict[str, List[str]] = {}
    unreadable: List[str] = []
    for lane in lanes:
        conn = open_bank(data_dir, lane)
        if conn is None:
            unreadable.append(lane)
            continue
        try:
            rows_by_lane[lane] = migrated_rows(conn)
            channels_by_lane[lane] = bank_channels(conn)
        finally:
            conn.close()

    all_rows = [row for rows in rows_by_lane.values() for row in rows]
    # A superseded row is history, not a memory the fleet holds. Every check
    # about what the store CLAIMS reads the live set; the checks about how
    # rows were written read every row, retired ones included.
    live_by_lane = {lane: [r for r in rows if not is_retired(r)] for lane, rows in rows_by_lane.items()}
    live_rows = [row for rows in live_by_lane.values() for row in rows]

    # 1 count reconciliation, per lane. A memory whose source file disappeared
    # is deliberately never retired, so the store legitimately holds it on top
    # of what this run derives. It reconciles as an accounted-for orphan and is
    # named in the detail, because the whole point of keeping it is that the
    # broken link stays visible.
    ledgers = {lane: read_ledger(data_dir, lane) for lane in lanes}
    orphaned = orphaned_ledger_keys(entries, lanes, ledgers)
    orphan_ids: Dict[str, set] = {}
    for item in orphaned:
        orphan_ids.setdefault(item["lane"], set()).add(item["memory_id"])
    mismatched = []
    kept_total = 0
    for lane in lanes:
        expected = len([e for e in entries if e.lane == lane])
        live = live_by_lane.get(lane, [])
        kept = [row for row in live if row["id"] in orphan_ids.get(lane, set())]
        kept_total += len(kept)
        if expected + len(kept) != len(live):
            mismatched.append(
                f"{lane}: derived {expected} plus {len(kept)} kept orphans, "
                f"store holds {len(live)} live")
    retired_total = len(all_rows) - len(live_rows)
    checks.append(check(
        "1 count reconciliation",
        not mismatched and not unreadable,
        "; ".join(mismatched + [f"{lane}: bank unreadable" for lane in unreadable]) or
        f"every lane matches ({len(entries)} live memories across {len(lanes)} lanes"
        f"{f', plus {kept_total} kept whose source file is gone' if kept_total else ''}"
        f"{f', plus {retired_total} superseded kept as history' if retired_total else ''})"))

    # 2 provenance completeness
    recorded = set()
    for row in live_rows:
        for path in row["metadata"].get("source_paths") or []:
            recorded.add(path)
    # A group whose text a canonical file already states is deliberately left
    # to that file's pointer, so its files are accounted for by the drop that
    # names their owner rather than missing from the store.
    dropped_paths = {path for item in dropped for path in item.get("source_paths") or []}
    unrouted_paths = {str(f.path) for f in files if not f.lane}
    # Renaming a file does not rewrite the memory it already produced: the
    # store's own dedupe hands back the row that holds that exact text, and
    # that row still records the path the file had when it was migrated. The
    # ledger says which live row the file's memory landed in, so the file is
    # held by the store under its former name rather than missing from it.
    live_ids = {lane: {row["id"] for row in rows} for lane, rows in live_by_lane.items()}
    renamed = set()
    for entry in entries:
        _, record = ledger_record_for(ledgers.get(entry.lane) or {}, entry)
        memory_id = record.get("memory_id")
        if memory_id and memory_id in live_ids.get(entry.lane, set()):
            renamed.update(path for path in entry.metadata["source_paths"] if path not in recorded)
    routed_paths = {str(f.path) for f in files if f.lane} - dropped_paths - renamed
    missing = sorted(routed_paths - recorded)
    checks.append(check(
        "2 provenance completeness",
        not missing,
        f"{len(missing)} routed source files are in no memory's provenance: {', '.join(missing[:5])}"
        if missing else
        f"all {len(routed_paths)} routed source files appear in a memory's provenance "
        f"({len(unrouted_paths)} unrouted and {len(dropped_paths)} left to a canonical "
        f"pointer, reported separately"
        f"{f'; {len(renamed)} held under the name they carried when migrated' if renamed else ''})"))

    # 3 no silent loss to the 24h trim
    unpinned = [row["id"] for row in all_rows if not row["consolidated_at"]]
    checks.append(check(
        "3 no silent loss to trim",
        not unpinned,
        f"{len(unpinned)} migrated memories are unpinned and would be trimmed" if unpinned
        else f"all {len(all_rows)} migrated memories are pinned"))

    # 4 scope correctness
    non_global = [row["id"] for row in all_rows if row["scope"] != "global"]
    checks.append(check(
        "4 scope correctness",
        not non_global,
        f"{len(non_global)} migrated memories are not global" if non_global
        else f"all {len(all_rows)} migrated memories are global"))

    # 5 and 6 embedding and FTS parity, over the memories this migration wrote.
    # A bank also holds rows nobody here wrote - phase 2's pointers, whatever
    # the lane already had - and their indexing is the store's business, not
    # this checklist's; counting the whole bank makes someone else's gap read
    # as a memory of ours that recall cannot reach.
    embedding_problems: List[str] = []
    fts_problems: List[str] = []
    for lane in lanes:
        ids = [row["id"] for row in rows_by_lane.get(lane, [])]
        if not ids:
            continue
        conn = open_bank(data_dir, lane)
        if conn is None:
            continue
        try:
            embedded = count_indexed(conn, "memory_embeddings", "memory_id", ids)
            indexed = count_indexed(conn, "fts_working", "id", ids)
        finally:
            conn.close()
        if embedded is None:
            embedding_problems.append(f"{lane}: the embedding table could not be read")
        elif embedded != len(ids):
            embedding_problems.append(
                f"{lane}: {len(ids) - embedded} of {len(ids)} migrated memories have no embedding")
        if indexed is None:
            fts_problems.append(f"{lane}: the FTS index could not be read")
        elif indexed != len(ids):
            fts_problems.append(
                f"{lane}: {len(ids) - indexed} of {len(ids)} migrated memories are not in the FTS index")
    checks.append(check("5 embedding parity", not embedding_problems,
                        "; ".join(embedding_problems)
                        or f"every one of {len(all_rows)} migrated memories carries an embedding"))
    checks.append(check("6 FTS parity", not fts_problems,
                        "; ".join(fts_problems)
                        or f"every one of {len(all_rows)} migrated memories is in its bank's FTS index"))

    # 7 clean lifecycle slate. A fresh migration retires nothing at all. A
    # re-run over an edited file supersedes, which is the point of superseding,
    # so what must hold is not "nothing is retired" but "nothing was retired
    # without something live taking its place" - the shape a lost memory would
    # actually have.
    # A second edit of one file builds a chain A -> B -> C in which B is itself
    # retired, so the only row naming A is a retired one. Following the chain is
    # what tells "replaced twice" from "replaced by nothing", which is the loss
    # this check exists to catch.
    successor = {}
    for row in all_rows:
        replaces = row["metadata"].get("supersedes")
        if replaces:
            successor[replaces] = row["id"]
    by_id = {row["id"]: row for row in all_rows}
    orphan_retired = []
    for row in all_rows:
        if not is_retired(row):
            continue
        seen_ids = {row["id"]}
        current = successor.get(row["id"])
        while current and current not in seen_ids:
            seen_ids.add(current)
            replacement = by_id.get(current)
            if replacement is None or not is_retired(replacement):
                break
            current = successor.get(current)
        replacement = by_id.get(current) if current else None
        if replacement is None or is_retired(replacement):
            orphan_retired.append(row["id"])
    retired_count = len(all_rows) - len(live_rows)
    checks.append(check(
        "7 clean lifecycle slate",
        not orphan_retired,
        f"{len(orphan_retired)} migrated memories are retired with nothing live replacing them: "
        f"{', '.join(orphan_retired[:5])}" if orphan_retired
        else (f"{retired_count} superseded, each replaced by a live memory" if retired_count
              else "no migrated memory is superseded or expired")))

    # 8 recall spot-check: every memory found by its own description, which is
    # never part of its stored text, so this is a paraphrase and not a substring.
    checks.append(recall_spot_check(live_by_lane, data_dir, home))

    # 9 lane containment
    # A row this lane's own ledger claims was written here by this tool, which
    # answers the provenance question even when its source file is long gone.
    ledger_ids = {lane: {(record or {}).get("memory_id") for record in (ledgers.get(lane) or {}).values()}
                  for lane in lanes}
    checks.append(lane_containment(live_by_lane, channels_by_lane, lane_of_source_path(entries),
                                   orphan_ids, ledger_ids, data_dir, home))

    # 10 cross-lane awareness
    checks.append(awareness_check(live_by_lane, data_dir, home))

    # 11 idempotence is proved by re-running `write`; what verify can prove is
    # that the store holds no second live row for one source file.
    # Keyed per lane: re-registering a project onto another lane leaves its old
    # rows in the old bank, because this tool never retires them, so one source
    # file legitimately has a live memory in two banks. Two live rows for it in
    # ONE bank is the duplicate this check is looking for.
    # A row's own metadata records the paths it was written from and is never
    # rewritten afterwards, so a renamed file leaves a stale path on a live row
    # while the ledger tracks where that memory is sourced now. The ledger is
    # therefore what a path is read from; a row the ledger does not know falls
    # back to its own metadata, which is what a stray duplicate looks like.
    current_paths: Dict[str, Dict[str, Set[str]]] = {}
    for lane in lanes:
        for record in (ledgers.get(lane) or {}).values():
            memory_id = (record or {}).get("memory_id")
            if memory_id:
                current_paths.setdefault(lane, {}).setdefault(memory_id, set()).update(
                    record.get("source_paths") or [])
    duplicated = []
    seen: Dict[Tuple[str, str], str] = {}
    for lane, rows in sorted(live_by_lane.items()):
        for row in rows:
            paths = current_paths.get(lane, {}).get(row["id"])
            if paths is None:
                paths = {p for p in [row["metadata"].get("source_path")] if p}
            for path in sorted(paths):
                if (lane, path) in seen:
                    duplicated.append(
                        f"{lane}: {path} is claimed by {seen[(lane, path)]} and {row['id']}")
                else:
                    seen[(lane, path)] = row["id"]
    checks.append(check(
        "11 no duplicate live rows per source",
        not duplicated,
        "; ".join(duplicated[:5]) or f"each of {len(seen)} source files has exactly one live memory"))

    # 13 Hermes untouched. The property is that nothing this migration writes
    # is in Hermes's own bank. Its row counts and mtime move constantly for
    # reasons of its own, so they are context here and never a verdict.
    snapshot = hermes_snapshot(data_dir)
    intrusion = hermes_intrusion(data_dir)
    if not intrusion.get("present"):
        untouched = True
        detail = "this data dir has no Hermes bank; there was nothing to disturb"
    elif intrusion.get("error"):
        untouched = False
        detail = f"the Hermes bank could not be read to prove it holds nothing of ours: {intrusion['error']}"
    elif intrusion.get("rows"):
        untouched = False
        detail = (f"{intrusion['rows']} rows in the Hermes bank carry this migration's "
                  f"provenance: {', '.join(intrusion.get('ids') or [])}")
    else:
        untouched = True
        detail = ("the Hermes bank holds no memory this migration wrote "
                  f"(it currently holds {snapshot.get('working_memory', 'unknown')} of its own)")
    checks.append(check("13 Hermes bank untouched by the migration", untouched, detail))

    # 14 sources intact. A file the scan could not read never became a
    # SourceFile, so the manifest cannot see it; this check would claim every
    # source is readable while the unreadable ones were quietly dropped.
    manifest = source_manifest(files)
    broken = [path for path, record in manifest.items() if "error" in record]
    broken += [problem["path"] for problem in problems]
    checks.append(check(
        "14 every source file still readable on disk",
        not broken,
        f"{len(broken)} source files could not be read: {', '.join(sorted(broken)[:5])}" if broken
        else f"all {len(manifest)} source files are intact and were only ever read"))

    # 15 nothing derived was refused and left unwritten. A refusal is the one
    # failure the rest of this checklist can absorb: the count reconciles
    # against the row that is still there and the provenance check excuses the
    # path that row still records, so a store that permanently fails to hold a
    # memory can read as a clean migration. This asks the question directly,
    # per derived memory, and answers it from the bank rather than the report.
    # Whether the bank holds a memory is `holds`, and nothing else. What
    # separates the ways it can be absent is read, never inferred, and named
    # four ways: a store refusal a re-run cannot clear, a transport failure a
    # re-run does clear, a memory never migrated at all, and a source that has
    # simply moved on since the last write. Only the last is a notice.
    refused_by_lane = {lane: read_refusals(data_dir, lane) for lane in lanes}
    unwritten = []
    stale = []
    try:
        for entry in entries:
            rows = live_by_lane.get(entry.lane) or []
            record = ledger_record_for(ledgers.get(entry.lane) or {}, entry)[1]
            name = entry.metadata.get("source_name") or entry.key
            landed = [row for row in rows if holds(row, entry)]
            if landed:
                if not any(row["channel_id"] == entry.channel_id for row in landed):
                    unwritten.append(
                        f"{entry.lane}: {name} is held under channel "
                        f"{landed[0]['channel_id']!r} but this run derives it for "
                        f"{entry.channel_id!r}")
                continue
            refusal = refused_for(refused_by_lane.get(entry.lane) or {}, entry)
            code = refusal.get("code", "unknown") if refusal else ""
            if refusal and code in TRANSPORT_CODES:
                unwritten.append(
                    f"{entry.lane}: {name} NEVER REACHED the store ({code}): the bridge "
                    f"for this lane failed, so re-running write clears it")
            elif refusal:
                unwritten.append(
                    f"{entry.lane}: {name} was REFUSED by the store "
                    f"({code}); re-running write is refused again, "
                    f"so this one needs a person")
            elif not record:
                unwritten.append(
                    f"{entry.lane}: {name} has NEVER BEEN MIGRATED - no ledger record "
                    f"and nothing in the bank holds it; run write")
            elif record.get("content_sha") == entry.content_sha:
                unwritten.append(
                    f"{entry.lane}: {name} is recorded in the ledger as written, "
                    f"but the store does not hold it")
            else:
                stale.append(
                    f"{entry.lane}: {name} has changed since the last migration wrote it; "
                    f"re-run write")
        blocked = ""
    except MigrateError as exc:
        unwritten, stale, blocked = [], [], str(exc)
    checks.append(check(
        "15 every derived memory landed in the store",
        not unwritten and not blocked,
        blocked or
        ((f"{len(unwritten)} derived memories were refused or never written: "
          f"{'; '.join(unwritten[:5])}" if unwritten else
          f"all {len(entries) - len(stale)} derived memories are live in their own lane "
          f"and channel")
         + (f"; {len(stale)} sources have moved on since the last write and are "
            f"listed below" if stale else ""))))

    review = []
    for lane in lanes:
        conn = open_bank(data_dir, lane)
        if conn is None:
            continue
        try:
            pairs = near_duplicates(conn, [row["id"] for row in live_by_lane.get(lane, [])])
        finally:
            conn.close()
        for pair in pairs:
            review.append(dict(pair, lane=lane))

    return {
        "checks": checks,
        "near_duplicate_review": review,
        "orphaned_ledger_keys": orphaned,
        "stale_sources": stale,
        "hermes": snapshot,
        "hermes_run_record": read_hermes_record(data_dir),
        "lanes": lanes,
    }


def recall_spot_check(rows_by_lane: Dict[str, List[Dict[str, Any]]], data_dir: Path,
                      home: Path) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    hits = 0
    total = 0
    misses: List[str] = []
    problems: List[str] = []
    for lane, rows in sorted(rows_by_lane.items()):
        # A description that was itself credential-bearing is the same constant
        # string on every pointer that lost one, so it identifies no row and
        # asking recall to find one by it proves nothing about the migration.
        candidates = [r for r in rows
                      if (r["metadata"].get("description") or "").strip()
                      and r["metadata"].get("description") != REDACTED_FIELD]
        if not candidates:
            continue
        try:
            session = BridgeSession(lane, data_dir, home)
        except (MigrateError, OSError) as exc:
            problems.append(f"{lane}: {exc}")
            continue
        try:
            for row in candidates:
                total += 1
                arguments: Dict[str, Any] = {
                    "query": row["metadata"]["description"],
                    "top_k": 5,
                    "include_awareness": False,
                }
                if row["channel_id"] != LANE_CHANNEL:
                    arguments["project"] = row["channel_id"]
                ok, payload = session.call_tool("memory_recall", arguments)
                found = ok and any(r.get("id") == row["id"] for r in payload.get("results") or [])
                if found:
                    hits += 1
                else:
                    misses.append(f"{lane}/{row['id']}: {row['metadata'].get('source_name', '')}")
        finally:
            session.close()
    if not problems and total == 0:
        # Every migrated memory here is one whose description was itself
        # credential-bearing, or carries none at all. There is nothing a
        # paraphrase probe could ask for, which is not the same as a miss.
        return check("8 recall spot-check by paraphrase", True,
                     "no migrated memory carries a description to spot-check by")
    ratio = (hits / total) if total else 0.0
    return check(
        "8 recall spot-check by paraphrase",
        not problems and total > 0 and ratio >= 0.9,
        f"{hits}/{total} memories returned in the top 5 for their own description"
        + (f"; missed: {', '.join(misses[:5])}" if misses else "")
        + (f"; {'; '.join(problems[:3])}" if problems else ""))


def distinctive_terms(rows: Sequence[Dict[str, Any]], other_rows: Sequence[Dict[str, Any]]) -> List[str]:
    """Words this lane uses that no other lane's migrated text contains."""
    def tokens(items: Sequence[Dict[str, Any]]) -> set:
        out = set()
        for row in items:
            for word in re.split(r"[^A-Za-z0-9]+", row["content"].lower()):
                if len(word) >= 8:
                    out.add(word)
        return out
    return sorted(tokens(rows) - tokens(other_rows))[:3]


def entitled_to(lane: str, row: Dict[str, Any], lane_of_path: Dict[str, str]) -> bool:
    """Whether this lane's bank is where this row belongs."""
    recorded = row["metadata"].get("lane")
    if recorded:
        return recorded == lane
    paths = row["metadata"].get("source_paths") or []
    return bool(paths) and all(lane_of_path.get(path) == lane for path in paths)


def lane_of_source_path(entries: Sequence[Entry]) -> Dict[str, str]:
    """Which lane this run derived each source file for.

    A row's own metadata records the source files it was derived from, and
    those files route to exactly one lane. That is what separates a lane's
    legitimate cross-lane duplicate - derived FOR this lane from this lane's
    own files - from a row carrying another lane's provenance.
    """
    out: Dict[str, str] = {}
    for entry in entries:
        for path in entry.metadata.get("source_paths") or []:
            out[path] = entry.lane
    return out


def misrouted_rows(lane: str, rows: Sequence[Dict[str, Any]],
                   lane_of_path: Dict[str, str], kept_orphans: Set[str],
                   ledger_ids: Set[str]) -> Tuple[List[str], List[str]]:
    """(leaks, residue) - rows in this bank whose provenance is another lane's.

    A row records the lane it was derived for, so this holds whether or not
    its source file still exists on disk. Rows the never-retire rule is
    deliberately keeping are residue rather than leaks: a project re-registered
    onto another lane leaves its old rows behind by design.
    """
    leaks: List[str] = []
    residue: List[str] = []
    unresolved: List[str] = []
    for row in rows:
        recorded = row["metadata"].get("lane")
        paths = row["metadata"].get("source_paths") or []
        claimed = {lane_of_path[path] for path in paths if path in lane_of_path}
        if recorded:
            claimed.add(recorded)
        elsewhere = sorted(claimed - {lane})
        if not elsewhere:
            if recorded == lane or any(lane_of_path.get(path) == lane for path in paths):
                continue
            # Nothing left on disk says where this row came from. A row the
            # never-retire rule is deliberately keeping is accounted for; any
            # other is a row whose containment cannot be shown either way.
            if row["id"] in kept_orphans:
                residue.append(row["id"])
            elif row["id"] not in ledger_ids:
                unresolved.append(f"{lane} holds {row['id']} and nothing records which lane it came from")
            continue
        if row["id"] in kept_orphans:
            # Re-registering a project onto another lane leaves its old rows
            # behind, because this tool never retires them. That residue is
            # the never-retire rule working, not a live cross-lane write.
            residue.append(row["id"])
            continue
        leaks.append(f"{lane} holds {row['id']}, derived for lane {', '.join(elsewhere)}")
    return (leaks + unresolved, residue)


def lane_containment(rows_by_lane: Dict[str, List[Dict[str, Any]]],
                     channels_by_lane: Dict[str, List[str]],
                     lane_of_path: Dict[str, str], kept_orphans: Dict[str, Set[str]],
                     ledger_ids: Dict[str, Set[str]], data_dir: Path,
                     home: Path) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    leaks: List[str] = []
    residue: List[str] = []
    seen: set = set()
    probes = 0
    for lane, rows in sorted(rows_by_lane.items()):
        lane_leaks, lane_residue = misrouted_rows(
            lane, rows, lane_of_path, kept_orphans.get(lane) or set(),
            ledger_ids.get(lane) or set())
        leaks.extend(lane_leaks)
        residue.extend(lane_residue)
    for lane, rows in sorted(rows_by_lane.items()):
        others = [r for other, rs in rows_by_lane.items() if other != lane for r in rs]
        # A group spanning two lanes is deliberately written once in EACH, so
        # this lane's own row can carry another lane's text by design. What
        # makes that row legitimate is its provenance - it was derived FOR this
        # lane - not the fact that it sits in this bank. Only those rows are
        # subtracted from the probe: a row this lane is not entitled to hold
        # must not be able to hide its own words from the term that finds it.
        entitled = [r for r in rows if entitled_to(lane, r, lane_of_path)]
        entitled_ids = {r["id"] for r in entitled}
        terms = distinctive_terms(others, entitled)
        if not terms:
            continue
        # The probe reads the whole bank, which holds memories this migration
        # never wrote - a phase-2 canonical pointer, say. A hit is a leak only
        # when what came back IS another lane's migrated memory, so the test is
        # that row's own identity and text, never the term alone.
        foreign_ids = {r["id"] for r in others}
        foreign_text = {normalize(r["content"]) for r in others}
        try:
            session = BridgeSession(lane, data_dir, home)
        except (MigrateError, OSError) as exc:
            leaks.append(f"{lane}: {exc}")
            continue
        probe_channels: List[Optional[str]] = [
            channel for channel in channels_by_lane.get(lane, []) if channel != LANE_CHANNEL]
        probe_channels.append(None)
        try:
            for term in terms:
                for channel in probe_channels:
                    probes += 1
                    arguments: Dict[str, Any] = {
                        "query": term, "top_k": 10, "include_awareness": False}
                    if channel:
                        arguments["project"] = channel
                    ok, payload = session.call_tool("memory_recall", arguments)
                    if not ok:
                        continue
                    for result in payload.get("results") or []:
                        content = result.get("content") or ""
                        if term not in set(re.split(r"[^a-z0-9]+", content.lower())):
                            continue
                        if result.get("id") in entitled_ids:
                            continue
                        if result.get("id") in foreign_ids or normalize(content) in foreign_text:
                            leak = (f"{lane} returned another lane's memory for term {term!r} "
                                    f"({result.get('id')})")
                            if leak not in seen:
                                seen.add(leak)
                                leaks.append(leak)
        finally:
            session.close()
    accounted = (f"; {len(residue)} rows kept in a lane their project no longer routes to, "
                 f"reported as orphaned rather than retired" if residue else "")
    return check(
        "9 lane containment",
        not leaks,
        "; ".join(leaks[:5]) + accounted if leaks else
        f"{probes} cross-lane term probes returned nothing from another lane{accounted}")


def awareness_check(rows_by_lane: Dict[str, List[Dict[str, Any]]], data_dir: Path,
                    home: Path) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    populated = sorted(lane for lane, rows in rows_by_lane.items() if rows)
    if len(populated) < 2:
        return check("10 cross-lane awareness", True, "fewer than two populated lanes; nothing to be aware of")
    missing: List[str] = []
    capped = False
    for lane in populated:
        result = subprocess.run(  # noqa: S603
            [sys.executable, str(BRIDGE), "awareness", "--exclude", lane, "--data-dir", str(data_dir)],
            env=dict(os.environ, FM_HOME=str(home)),
            capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)
        try:
            payload = json.loads(result.stdout)
        except ValueError:
            missing.append(f"{lane}: awareness did not answer ({result.stderr.strip()})")
            continue
        seen = {title.get("lane") for title in payload.get("titles") or []}
        if payload.get("count", 0) >= 15:
            capped = True
        for other in populated:
            if other != lane and other not in seen:
                missing.append(f"from {lane}, lane {other} contributes no title")
    detail = "; ".join(missing[:5]) or f"every populated lane sees every other ({len(populated)} lanes)"
    if missing and capped:
        detail += "; the index is at its 15-title cap, so lower-importance lanes are crowded out"
    return check("10 cross-lane awareness", not missing, detail)


# --- rendering ---------------------------------------------------------------


def entry_json(entry: Entry) -> Dict[str, Any]:
    return {
        "key": entry.key,
        "lane": entry.lane,
        "channel_id": entry.channel_id,
        "memory_type": entry.memory_type,
        "importance": entry.importance,
        "veracity": entry.veracity,
        "source": entry.source,
        "content": entry.content,
        "metadata": entry.metadata,
        "notes": entry.notes,
    }


def render_plan(plan: Dict[str, Any], files: Sequence[SourceFile], problems: Sequence[Dict[str, str]]) -> None:
    entries: List[Entry] = plan["entries"]
    by_lane: Dict[str, List[Entry]] = {}
    for entry in entries:
        by_lane.setdefault(entry.lane, []).append(entry)
    pointers = [e for e in entries if e.source == POINTER_SOURCE]
    print(f"sources read: {len(files)} files in "
          f"{len({f.dir_name for f in files})} memory directories")
    print(f"memories derived: {len(entries)} across {len(by_lane)} lanes "
          f"({len(pointers)} credential pointers)")
    for lane in sorted(by_lane):
        lane_entries = by_lane[lane]
        print(f"\n{lane} ({len(lane_entries)})")
        for entry in sorted(lane_entries, key=lambda e: e.key):
            marker = "POINTER" if entry.source == POINTER_SOURCE else entry.memory_type
            note = f"  [{'; '.join(entry.notes)}]" if entry.notes else ""
            print(f"  {entry.channel_id:24s} {marker:12s} {entry.metadata['source_name']}{note}")
    if pointers:
        print("\ncredential pointers (content NEVER copied):")
        for entry in sorted(pointers, key=lambda e: e.key):
            reasons = "; ".join(
                f"{f['rule']} ({f['evidence']})" for f in entry.metadata["credential_findings"][:3])
            print(f"  {entry.metadata['source_path']}\n      {reasons}")
    if plan["cross_lane_duplicates"]:
        print("\nidentical content in more than one lane (written once per lane, never merged):")
        for item in plan["cross_lane_duplicates"]:
            print(f"  {item['name']}: {', '.join(item['lanes'])}")
    if plan["dropped_for_pointer"]:
        print("\ndropped in favour of a canonical-file pointer:")
        for item in plan["dropped_for_pointer"]:
            print(f"  {item['name']} -> {item['owner']}")
    if plan["partial_pointer_overlaps"]:
        print("\npartly stated by a canonical file already - migrated anyway, reported for review:")
        for item in plan["partial_pointer_overlaps"]:
            print(f"  {item['name']} ~ {item['owner']} ({int(item['overlap'] * 100)}% of its words)")
    canonicalised = canonicalised_projects(files)
    if canonicalised:
        print("\ncanonicalised - one project assembled from several memory directories:")
        for item in canonicalised:
            print(f"  {item['project']} ({item['lane']}): {item['files']} files from "
                  f"{len(item['directories'])} directories: {', '.join(item['directories'])}")
    unrouted: List[SourceFile] = plan["unrouted"]
    if unrouted:
        print(f"\nUNROUTED ({len(unrouted)} files) - nothing is written for these:")
        for dir_name in sorted({f.dir_name for f in unrouted}):
            members = [f for f in unrouted if f.dir_name == dir_name]
            project = members[0].project
            why = (f"project {project!r} carries no lane in data/projects.md" if project
                   else "matches no registered project")
            print(f"  {dir_name} ({len(members)} files): {why}")
    fragments = unrouted_fragments(files)
    if fragments:
        print("\nunrouted directories that look like ONE project - an observation, not a route;")
        print("registering the project in data/projects.md with a lane is what would migrate them:")
        for item in fragments:
            print(f"  {item['looks_like_project']} ({item['files']} files): "
                  f"{', '.join(item['directories'])}")
    for problem in problems:
        print(f"\nUNREADABLE {problem['path']}: {problem['problem']}")


def render_checks(result: Dict[str, Any]) -> bool:
    ok = True
    for item in result["checks"]:
        mark = "PASS" if item["pass"] else "FAIL"
        ok = ok and item["pass"]
        print(f"{mark}  {item['check']}: {item['detail']}")
    for orphan in result.get("orphaned_ledger_keys") or []:
        print(f"ORPHANED {orphan['lane']}/{orphan['key']} "
              f"(memory {orphan['memory_id']} kept; retiring it is a deliberate act)")
    for item in result.get("stale_sources") or []:
        print(f"STALE {item}")
    review = result["near_duplicate_review"]
    if review:
        print(f"\nnear-duplicate review ({len(review)} pairs at cosine >= {NEAR_DUPLICATE_COSINE}, "
              f"never merged automatically):")
        for pair in review[:20]:
            print(f"  {pair['lane']}: {pair['a']} ~ {pair['b']} ({pair['cosine']})")
    return ok


# --- CLI ---------------------------------------------------------------------


def main(argv: Optional[List[str]] = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="fm-memory-migrate",
        description="Mirror Claude Code's auto-memory files into the Mnemosyne lane banks.")
    parser.add_argument("command", choices=["plan", "write", "verify"])
    parser.add_argument("--source", help=f"auto-memory root (default: {DEFAULT_SOURCE_ROOT})")
    parser.add_argument("--home", help="firstmate home holding data/projects.md (default: FM_HOME)")
    parser.add_argument("--data-dir", help=f"Mnemosyne data dir (default: {DEFAULT_DATA_DIR})")
    parser.add_argument("--json", action="store_true", help="machine-readable output")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    home = Path(args.home or os.environ.get("FM_HOME") or Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent)
    source_root = Path(args.source) if args.source else DEFAULT_SOURCE_ROOT
    data_dir = Path(args.data_dir) if args.data_dir else Path(
        os.environ.get("MNEMOSYNE_DATA_DIR") or DEFAULT_DATA_DIR)

    try:
        files, problems = scan_sources(source_root, home)
    except MigrateError as exc:
        print(f"fm-memory-migrate: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    plan = build_entries(files, home)
    entries: List[Entry] = plan["entries"]
    unrouted: List[SourceFile] = plan["unrouted"]

    if args.command == "plan":
        if args.json:
            print(json.dumps({
                "sources_read": len(files),
                "entries": [entry_json(e) for e in entries],
                "credential_pointers": [entry_json(e) for e in entries if e.source == POINTER_SOURCE],
                "cross_lane_duplicates": plan["cross_lane_duplicates"],
                "dropped_for_pointer": plan["dropped_for_pointer"],
                "partial_pointer_overlaps": plan["partial_pointer_overlaps"],
                "unrouted": [{"path": str(f.path), "dir": f.dir_name, "project": f.project} for f in unrouted],
                "unrouted_fragments": unrouted_fragments(files),
                "canonicalised_projects": canonicalised_projects(files),
                "unreadable": list(problems),
            }, indent=2))
        else:
            render_plan(plan, files, problems)
        return 1 if (unrouted or problems) else 0

    if args.command == "write":
        before = source_manifest(files)
        hermes_before = hermes_snapshot(data_dir)
        result = write_migration(entries, data_dir, home)
        after = source_manifest(files)
        hermes_after = hermes_snapshot(data_dir)
        write_hermes_record(data_dir, hermes_before, hermes_after)
        drift = manifest_drift(before, after)
        result["counts"]["credential_pointers"] = len([e for e in entries if e.source == POINTER_SOURCE])
        result["counts"]["unrouted"] = len(unrouted)
        result["counts"]["dropped_for_pointer"] = len(plan["dropped_for_pointer"])
        result["source_drift"] = drift
        result["hermes_before"] = hermes_before
        result["hermes_after"] = hermes_after
        intrusion = hermes_intrusion(data_dir)
        result["hermes_intrusion"] = intrusion
        hermes_ok = not intrusion.get("rows") and not intrusion.get("error")
        if args.json:
            print(json.dumps(dict(result, hermes_uncontaminated=hermes_ok), indent=2))
        else:
            counts = result["counts"]
            print(" ".join(f"{k}={v}" for k, v in sorted(counts.items())))
            for lane in result["lanes"]:
                print(f"  {lane['lane']}: {json.dumps(lane)}")
            for refusal in result["refusals"]:
                print(f"REFUSED {refusal['key']}: {refusal['code']} - {refusal['message']}")
            for orphan in result["orphaned_ledger_keys"]:
                print(f"ORPHANED {orphan['lane']}/{orphan['key']} "
                      f"(memory {orphan['memory_id']} kept; retiring it is a deliberate act)")
            print(f"sources unchanged: {'yes' if not drift else 'NO - ' + ', '.join(drift)}")
            print(f"Hermes bank holds nothing this migration wrote: "
                  f"{'yes' if hermes_ok else 'NO - ' + json.dumps(intrusion)}")
        if drift:
            print("fm-memory-migrate: a source file changed during the run; mirror mode is violated",
                  file=sys.stderr)
        failed = bool(drift) or not hermes_ok or result["counts"]["refused"] or unrouted
        return 1 if failed else 0

    result = verify(entries, files, data_dir, home, plan["dropped_for_pointer"], problems)
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps(result, indent=2))
        return 0 if all(c["pass"] for c in result["checks"]) else 1
    return 0 if render_checks(result) else 1


if __name__ == "__main__":
    try:
        sys.exit(main())
    except MigrateError as exc:
        print(f"fm-memory-migrate: {exc}", file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    except KeyboardInterrupt:
        sys.exit(130)
